{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content project-trajectory/PROCESS.md | Select-Object -Skip 903 -First 80; git show d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^'\"\\\\[need.SN-(012|036)\\\\]' -Context 0,12; git show d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^'\"\\\\[interface.IF-(115|113|175|287)\\\\]' -Context 0,10\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content project-trajectory/PROCESS.md | Select-Object -Skip 903 -First 80; git show d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^'\"\\\\[need.SN-(012|036)\\\\]' -Context 0,12; git show d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^'\"\\\\[interface.IF-(115|113|175|287)\\\\]' -Context 0,10\"","aggregated_output":"## 5. Verdict & status protocol\r\n\r\nTwo files split *now* from *history*: `status.md` is the **working surface** —\r\nthe whole file holds only what must be performed next (open items, pending\r\ndecisions, the next action) — and `log.md` is the **append-only history** it\r\npoints at (sign-offs, verdicts, approved decisions, session notes; evidence,\r\nnever normative). Act from status.md; append evidence to log.md — directly on\r\nthe serial trunk lane, or as a `docs/log.d/<WI-id>-<slug>.md` fragment on a\r\nwork branch, compiled into `log.md` in merge order by `trunk_step.py` (the\r\nsign-off table and Decisions log are trunk-serial edits, never fragments).\r\nThere is a **third** tier behind those two — `docs/archive/`, where a document\r\nthat records a historical decision and is no longer read by a script goes to\r\nstop competing with the live surfaces; its README states the boundary rule and\r\nthe counter-rule (a generated or script-read surface is machinery, not history).\r\nA retired spine row is deleted and leaves one record, `docs/log.d/retired/<ID>.md`\r\n(id, date, successor, reason), written by `retire.py` with the deletion. Records\r\nare for **lookup, not browsing**: read the record of a spent id you meet; never\r\nsurvey the set.\r\n\r\n**Open items — the owner decision surface, always shipped.** A decision deferred\r\nto the owner is a **row** in `docs/requirements/open-items.toml`, rendered by\r\n`gen_open_items.py` into `docs/open-items.html`; status.md's `Needs <human>`\r\nbullets stay one-liners pointing at it. A work item that waits on a decision\r\ncites it in its `needs` and reads `blocked`; every pending item is filed with\r\nthe queued row citing it, and ruling it updates that row in the same commit\r\n(IF-073 in the registry's header, IF-054 in `docs/work/README.md`). Every\r\nscaffold gets both whatever its\r\nprofile — *you deferred and no `OI` row resolves it* is a finding only a repo\r\nthat HAS the registry can act on. Three mechanisms keep the announcement and the\r\nqueue from being two artifacts: a `docs/provenance-allow` entry **names** the\r\n`OI-###` it defers (a required field, integrity-class); a `docs/log.d/` fragment\r\n**declares** the ids its session deferred (`Deferred open items: OI-45` / `…\r\nnone — <why>`), warn-only at the commit bar; and zero pending rows while entries\r\nstill stand is reported as a contradiction naming them. The surface is the\r\nsubstrate, never the mechanism — a fresh registry renders *the owner queue is\r\nempty* perfectly truthfully.\r\n\r\nReviews use this block — in `log.md`, or as a `docs/reviews/WI-<n>-<PHASE>.md`\r\nverdict file under the review layer (work-item-scoped names; serial counters\r\nrace under concurrency):\r\n\r\n```\r\n### <HAT or REVIEWER> — <Gate> — Round <r> — <YYYY-MM-DD>\r\nVERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=<N>\r\nFindings:\r\n- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <ID or area> → <issue> → <suggested change> → @<owner>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSitting sign-offs live in the **Sittings** table in `log.md`; the driver\r\nrecords the gate decision there, cites it from status.md's *Stage* line,\r\nand requests acceptance per the declared gate authority (§4).\r\n\r\n**Voice policy — warmth has a layer boundary.** Personality is a human value, not\r\na machine one: **human-facing** output (CLI narration, a kickoff greeting, a\r\nrelease-checklist intro) may carry warmth and **dry wit at most**;\r\n**machine/agent-facing** output — this protocol's findings and verdicts,\r\nsubagent prompts, registry cells, commit messages — stays **literal, terse,\r\nstructured: no whimsy**. Levity there costs tokens, reads ambiguously to the\r\nnext agent (irony/understatement is exactly what a parser misreads), and\r\nerodes the honesty/severity signal this protocol depends on. Default voice is\r\n**restrained**\r\n(\"direct and concrete; dry wit at most; never at the expense of clarity or\r\nhonesty\"); a project may expose an optional, named **tone knob** to dial levity\r\nup or down — never a baked-in persona, since no single tone fits both a\r\nmedical-device repo and a game studio.\r\n\r\n### Change intake — routing a problem to the spine\r\n\r\nAn inbound problem routes by **which registry row it contradicts** — that\r\nclassification, not the fix, is step 1. Then check the parent obligation and\r\nselected design before treating the violation as a coverage gap:\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n  P[\"problem identified\"] --> C{\"which row does it contradict?\"}\r\n  C -->|\"an existing SR/LLR is violated\"| D{\"obligation and design still sound?\"}\r\n  D -->|\"yes\"| TG[\"coverage gap:\\nno TC caught it\"]\r\n  D -->|\"parent sound; LLR unsuitable\"| DR[\"amend LLR + verification\\npreserve parent acceptance\"]\r\n  D -->|\"promised behavior changes\"| RG\r\n  C -->|\"no row speaks to it\"| RG[\"requirement gap:\\nnew/changed SN -> SR -> LLR\\n(walk DevStg-Reqs/DevStg-Tests for that slice)\"]\r\n\r\n> [need.SN-012]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-02\"]\r\n  tags = [\"process\"]\r\n  need =\"\"\"A team can keep small changes small: the process is **right-sized**, not ceremony for its own sake — small \r\nchanges stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"An over-heavy process is abandoned; adoption depends on proportionality.\"\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"Opt-in layers (perf, guardrails, unattended, parallel tracks, OKF export) cost a repo that doesn't \r\nuse them nothing; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-023]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\n  # Both tags are INERT AS ROUTING and kept as SUBJECT metadata. `ui`: the UX\r\n> [need.SN-036]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** When a stakeholder's need is turned into detailed \r\nrequirements, it is examined from every relevant expert and downstream-user perspective so important failure cases are \r\nnot lost.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"\"\"A decomposition produced from one perspective can omit security, operations, accessibility or first-use \r\nfailures while still appearing complete. The omission remains invisible unless the review records which relevant \r\nperspectives were considered and what each contributed.\"\"\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"Each decomposition round is reviewed against every applicable declared perspective, and the review \r\nrecord names each perspective and its outcome. The record demonstrates coverage and provenance; an independent review \r\njudges whether the resulting requirements are adequate.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-037]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\n  priority = \"M\"\r\n  need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A stakeholder can see where each promised system behavior \r\nenters or leaves the system, and the architecture changes with those promises instead of being invented afterward.\"\"\"\r\n> [interface.IF-175]\r\n  owner = \"scripts/kitlib/verdict\"\r\n  requestors = [\"scripts/integrate\", \"scripts/agent_loop\", \"scripts/dispatch\", \"scripts/gen_verdict_rollup\", \r\n\"scripts/score_reviews\", \"scripts/acceptance_record\", \"scripts/adjudicate_act\", \"scripts/review_brief\", \r\n\"scripts/adoption_record\"]\r\n  channel = \"call\"\r\n  data = \"tree identity; branch rounds/trailers; row rulings and blessed rows; scope, phase, filing and trailer \r\nreaders; the bar attestation grammar, verifier and tests digest; the review-attestation walk; the red record and the \r\nred-round count; a shared close-shape reader whose optional relink oracle admits only byte-exact inbound relinks, \r\nwhile the peel calls it without that oracle\"\r\n  version = \"v1\"\r\n  status = \"Drafted\"\r\n  component = \"CMP-008\"\r\n  notes = \"\"\"The seam exists because two callers ask ONE question from two sides - the merge slot's may-this-merge and \r\nthe loop's does-this-lane-still-owe-a-round - and answering it twice is what let a lane draw two identical rounds over \r\na verdict nothing had invalidated. So this row is not bookkeeping over an import: it is the declaration that there is \r\nexactly one definition, and a second reader of round evidence anywhere else is a finding against this row rather than \r\na new one. That definition includes the REV the identity is measured at, which is why the refresh peel is owned here \r\nand not by the merge slot: while it lived there, one reader peeled and the other measured at HEAD, and the row's whole \r\nclaim was false across exactly one commit class. The peel has TWO shapes on purpose and they must not be merged: \r\nwork_tip peels only a refresh sitting on the tip because it feeds a reset --hard where one commit too far destroys \r\nwork, while governing_rev walks through any commit whose non-record identity equals its first parent's, to reach a \r\ndisposable commit it would otherwise hide - a read-only question can afford an answer a destructive one cannot. Those \r\ntwo SHAPES are what this row governs; WHICH machine-authored commits are disposable is a class list, it belongs to the \r\nowner's own row (LLR-207 names both the station refresh and the machinery's adjudication close), and it is \r\ndeliberately not restated here, so a reader meeting one seam sentence and one design sentence is never choosing which \r\nis current. That step condition is the row's own sentence rather than a proxy for it, so no commit SHAPE gets a case \r\nhere: a walk that classified the paths a commit touched stopped at the zero-path ones it could not read, and the empty \r\ncommit an attestation rides when its bookkeeping is unchanged is that shape, so the commit recording an approval \r\nburied the refresh. The singularity binds the BINDINGS too, not only the two named readers: the identity a round \r\nfile's reviewed sha names and the identity an attestation's carrier names are asked through governing_identity at that \r\ncommit, because a raw fold is this composition with the peel dropped and it disagrees across exactly the commit class \r\nthe peel exists for - which is how a round drawn after a refresh became invisible to both readers at once, with \r\nnothing on the branch able to reconcile them. The rollup generator is a requestor for the NAME grammar only, and \r\nscore_reviews for the PHASE SPAN only - it holds a hard `from kitlib.verdict import declared_phases` so \r\nlatest_phase_verdicts keys the merge boundary's map by exactly the phases the loop schedules, and it reads no round \r\nevidence at all, so naming it here adds a requestor and NOT a fourth reader of the verdict. It is named because an \r\nimport no requestors entry carries is an undeclared seam, which is the completeness rule this cell answers to; the \r\nsingularity sentence above is about round evidence and does not reach a span reader either way. The span crosses as \r\nthe CONSTANT too, not only through declared_phases: agent_loop's non-build phase set and its dial ceiling ask it at \r\nmodule scope, and that module deliberately keeps no tuple of its own, so a phase added here reaches the scheduler, the \r\nrouting set and the clamp together. Off git every function answers None and this module never says what that means; \r\neach requestor declares its own direction, and both declared directions are toward more review. The gate-bearing round \r\ncontract crosses here too: format_scope records full, narrow or final review scope; gating_span admits a fresh full \r\nscope rooted at the claim base in either mode, admits final only with reviewer retention on and final_reviewer \r\npersisted, and admits neither narrow nor retained full scope; filing_refusal gives both filing and gate reading one \r\ninvalid-evidence boundary. dispatch is also a requestor, so the requestor list names every current reader.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [interface.IF-173]\r\n> [interface.IF-113]\r\n  rationale = \"\"\"The loader's both-ways strictness is load-bearing here rather than merely tidy: a judge's brief whose \r\nevidence section is thin reads as an investigation that was run and found nothing, so there is deliberately no \r\npartially-filled path across this seam.\"\"\"\r\n  owner = \"scripts/prompts\"\r\n  requestors = [\"scripts/adjudicate_brief\", \"scripts/amendment_brief\", \"scripts/adjudicate_act\"]\r\n  channel = \"call\"\r\n  data = \"ADJUDICATE-AMENDMENT · ADJUDICATE-DISPOSITION · ADJUDICATE-RED-TC · ADJUDICATE-DONE-WHEN · \r\nADJUDICATE-COMBINED · ADJUDICATE-ACT · PROMPTS / SPINE_QUESTIONS\"\r\n  version = \"v1\"\r\n  status = \"Drafted\"\r\n  component = \"CMP-008\"\r\n  notes = \"\"\"intra-component: both endpoints are CMP-008 under the narrow-waist partition, which puts the coordinator \r\nand the prompt loader in one unit, so the architecture rule no longer demands this row; it stands as the declared \r\nconsumption of the loader contract, whose provider side is IF-097. Sibling of IF-099 (the session engine's own use of \r\nthe same loader): the difference is that a failed fill here HOLDS the work item for a human rather than degrading, \r\nbecause the alternative brief would be the implementer's.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n> [interface.IF-115]\r\n  owner = \"scripts/adjudicate_brief\"\r\n  requestors = [\"scripts/agent_loop\"]\r\n  channel = \"call\"\r\n  data = \"compose(...) -> text | reason; record_outcome(...) -> (accepted | failed, why | None)\"\r\n  version = \"v1\"\r\n  status = \"Drafted\"\r\n  component = \"CMP-008\"\r\n  notes = \"intra-component (both CMP-008), declared because the fail-closed obligation lives on the CALLER and would \r\notherwise be recorded nowhere. `ROUTED` names all eight shipped briefs with an assembler; `conflict` is retired, with \r\nits missing derivation named in the module header. The judging composer derives no approval-registry token; the act \r\nbrief joins the retained identity of actable classes.\"\r\n  \r\n  [interface.IF-117]\r\n> [interface.IF-287]\r\n  rationale = \"Combined brief composition, review verdict checking, merge-time disposition and approval acts need one \r\ninterpretation of their machine lines, kind-scoped tokens, bound row identities and sections; separate parsers could \r\nlet one judgement act outside its requested scope or let a malformed review verdict reach a different consumer.\"\r\n  owner = \"scripts/kitlib/sitting\"\r\n  requestors = [\"scripts/adjudicate_brief\", \"scripts/amendment_brief\", \"scripts/acceptance_record\", \r\n\"scripts/consolidate\", \"scripts/intake\", \"scripts/review_brief\", \"scripts/score_reviews\", \"scripts/plan_coverage\", \r\n\"scripts/adjudicate_act\", \"scripts/held_status\"]\r\n  channel = \"call\"\r\n  data = \"binding/path grammar; requested, identity and findings-file cells; outcome; combined-scope and \r\nreview-verdict readers\"\r\n  version = \"v1\"\r\n  status = \"Drafted\"\r\n  component = \"CMP-008\"\r\n  notes = \"source - consumes no declared seam; pure functions. An identity cell is preserved when a pending binding is \r\nrewritten as accepted or failed; absent, malformed or repeated identities read as no identity with a reason. A \r\nfindings-file record is carried the same way when a pending binding is rewritten as accepted or failed; an absent \r\nrecord reads as none.\"\r\n  \r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$verdictPath = '\"'C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/review-tmp/2026-11-04-leg33/wi-983/WI-983-plan-critique-1.md'\n\"'$verdictText = @'\"'\nReviewed: d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824\n\n### Independent plan critic — WI-983 plan — Round 1 — 2026-11-04\n\nProvenance: fresh non-author critic session invoked for this review; this session did not produce the plan. The coordinator binds this sitting to the assigned verdict path above. No provider session id was supplied. Review used the text artifact obtained by the prescribed git-show recipe, not an image proxy. No implementer session notes, docs/status.md, docs/log.md, or neighboring plan critiques were used.\n\nRubric: docs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md at d4107e10b2acd2fd7cc3352e209f7cfe3758a824, including G5's referenced docs/rubrics/architecture-decomposition.md at that revision.\n\nIntent sources: docs/work/active/wi-983/WI-983-sitting-records-stopping-boundary.md in full and its specref docs/reviews/wi-976/021-ADJUDICATE-095bb79.md at the reviewed revision; SR-186 and SR-161 in docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml, with their parent SN-012 and SN-036 in docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml. Supporting contract checks used LLR-360, TC-399, the cited interface rows and WI-998's queued spec. The supplied coverage report was the coverage pre-pass.\n\nRubric derivation check: the rubric independently covers the intent at this artifact's planning tier. DevStg-Tests and B2 require actual delivery of the named per-chain stopping instruction and its bounded round classification, rather than merely citing a passing test. DevStg-Impl and B3 protect SN-012's proportionality; G5 checks the domain change; G4/B4 check the sequencing that separates this brief change from later inspection. The rubric does not substitute an automated line-presence test for SR-186's subjective stopping-reason judgment. No missing anchor was identified for this plan's intent.\n\nTransfer caveat: the debate/selection evidence comes from QA, mathematics and code with objective verifiers. Plan-artifact quality and two-planner reconciliation remain unbenchmarked; this rubric is an extrapolation, not a proven instrument.\n\nAnchor coverage and judgment:\n\n- DevStg-Reqs: P1-P7 name bounded deliverables and checkable outcomes. Their detailed sections make the trace edits, tests, composer change, resync instructions, adjudicated row text and final validation executable by a first-time builder.\n- DevStg-Tests: D1 is delivered through the round block, placement behavior and traced specification; D2 composes both actual shipped templates; D3 verifies the pre-claim successor filing and its required order; D4 supplies the resync entry and landing checks. The TC-178 and TC-198 exclusions preserve untouched hat mechanics. TC-211's exclusion follows the explicit successor split and preserves the failed observation until re-inspection.\n- DevStg-Impl: the repeated D1/D2 citations split trace, test, behavior and specification artifacts. D4 splits migration guidance from validation. The stated commit groupings explain why these steps are separate without implying independently landable intermediate states.\n- G4 and B4: the prerequisite check precedes mutation; trace cells and tests form one commit; behavior consumes those tests; behavior and resync form the next commit; the final row-text sitting consumes that settled change and the routed trace edits; landing validation follows the sitting. The P5-to-P6 edge is supported by the declared combined code/resync commit, not an extra independent resync-only review stage.\n- G5: DELTA: within is supported under A2/A3/A4/A6/A8. The plan carries the added instruction and non-ruling verdict line through P2/P4/P6, retaining existing round classification and parser semantics. Its dependency table uses existing classification inputs. Existing intra-component seams remain applicable; no new boundary or interface signature is proposed. The design states why the obligation stays with LLR-360.\n- B1: the plan consumes existing composer, loader and verdict-reader seams; it proposes no duplicate interface.\n- B2: each clause citation has a concrete contribution. The template test checks composed shipped text, so dispatcher-note edits alone cannot satisfy D2.\n- B3: no padding found. The no-change P1 is a check of an explicit Done-when prerequisite; the remaining steps have distinct artifacts or validation outcomes.\n\nFindings: none.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE findings=0\n'@\n[System.IO.Directory]::CreateDirectory([System.IO.Path]::GetDirectoryName(\"'$verdictPath)) | Out-Null\n[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($verdictPath, $verdictText + \"`n\", [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false))\nGet-Content -LiteralPath $verdictPath -First 1\nGet-Content -LiteralPath $verdictPath -Tail 1'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
